Let ${x_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x}}$ be the input to a $L$-layer linear feed-forward network with $n_1, \ldots, n_L = n_y$ units per layer. Let ${W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{n_1 \times n_x}, \ldots ,W_L \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y \times n_{L-1}}}$ be the weight matrices for each layer, ${b_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{n_1}, \ldots ,b_L \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y}}$ the bias vectors, and let ${\mathbf{w} = [vec(W_1)\\vec(b_1)\\vec(W_2)\\vec(b_2)\\\vdots] \in \mathbb{R}^n}$ for $n = \sum_{i=1}^L n_i(n_{i-1}+1)$ be the concatenation of all layer weights and biases. Let ${\gamma_0, \beta_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{n_x}, \ldots, \gamma_L, \beta_L \in \mathbb{R}^{n_y}}$ be the scale and offset parameters for each layer and ${\mathbf{u} = [vec(\gamma_0)\\vec(\beta_0)\\vec(\gamma_1\\vec(\beta_1)\\\vdots] \in \mathbb{R}^m}$ for $m = \sum_{i=0}^L n_i(n_i+1)$ be the concatenation of all such parameters. Layer normalization normalizes activations for each layer $i$ by the mean $\mu_i \in \mathbb{R}^{n_i}$ and standard deviation $\Sigma_i \in \mathbb{R}^{n_i \times n_i}$ of its activations. Then the network functions are

${f_i(x_i, \mathbf{u}, W_{i+1}) = \gamma_i \circ \frac{x_i - \mu_i}{\Sigma_i} + \beta_i}$

for $i = 1, \ldots, L-1$ and inputs

${\dot{y}_i(x_{i-1}, \mathbf{u}_i, W_i) = W_i[x_{i-1}\\1]}$

and outputs

${\ddot{y}_i(x_i, \mathbf{u}_i, W_{i+1}) = \sigma_i \circ \Gamma_i x_i + \sigma_i' \circ \Gamma_i \bar{x}_i + \bar{\gamma}_i \bar{x}_i }$,

so that $\ddot{y}_i = W_{i+1}[f_i(x_i, \mathbf{u}_i) \\ 1]$ and $\bar{x}_i = 1^Tx_i/n_i$.

By the theory of layer normalization, it follows that

${x_i = \tilde{\Gamma}_i\dot{y}_{i}}$

for ${\tilde{\Gamma}_i = diag(\Sigma^{-1}_i), x_i \not = \bar{x}_i}$ or

${x_i = (\tilde{\Gamma}'_i - \tilde{\Gamma}'_i \bar{x} \bar{x}^T)\dot{y}_{i}}$

for ${\tilde{\Gamma}'_i = diag(\Gamma_i^{-1}), x_i \not = \bar{x}_i}$.
