Quantization converts weights from high-precision (e.g. 64-bit floating-point) to lower-precision types (e.g. 8-bit signed integer) throughout the neural network while maintaining the same overall architecture, thus accelerating inference performance by making use of the smaller, more efficient integer instructions available on many platforms.

NVIDIA TensorRT also makes use of “hybrid” quantization during inference, in which arithmetic operations such as convolutions and fully-connected layers are performed in INT8 while critical operations that require higher dynamic range, such as layer normalization, are performed in FP16. When calibrating a model for 8-bit quantization mode, TensorRT keeps a higher precision copy of the weights and activations in FP16 for these layers.

