The output of the neuron is not defined by one mathematical function. Instead, SONs have three sets of coefficients associated with the same input variables: one for normal output, one for network control, and one for its own learning. A single and bi-mode hypernetworks comprising SONs is shown below.

Single and bi-model hypernetworks comprising SONs

In figure (b) above, it can be seen that there are some neurons that have no inputs from the outside of the system. These neurons are referred to as specialised neurons (SPN), since they only provide the functionality to process data to control the network.

Similar to how synaptic connections work between neurons in the brain, hypernetworks neural connections that transfer data from one neuron to another must have at least two different values assigned to them, as can be seen in the chart above. Neurons in the SPN cluster on the right of the chart can have less than two neural connections. The only condition is that networks input and output values can reach all SPNs.

Researchers tested their hypernetwork model on multi-class classification tasks using data from the University of California, Irvine machine learning repository. These varied from identifying handwritten digits of zeros to nines, to identifying cancer patients for speedy access to treatment. Their hypernetwork model outperformed all other current models in every scenario.

