The simplest way to think about sampling is as a wheel where all the words are different-sized slots weighted by their predicted probability. Roll this wheel as many times as you like for as many steps as you need to generate text in the wheel. It turns out that this wheel is really a multinomial random variable.

After you generate some amount of text off the wheel, you can control how likely the model is to output probabilities that are close to the ones it was trained on. The wheel has logits that the model predicts which represent the log of how likely the model thinks that something should happen, like a word should occur next. You always want a shallower log, because getting the wheel off-balance is how you get lots of unlikely text: either you make the counts less counts-like (denominator) or you make the logits (numerators) less likely (for instance, by applying a softmax on them). This is done by adjusting the temperature – a higher temperature means that the logits (which are normalized in the form of probabilities) are flatter, meaning the chance of landing in each one becomes approximately equal (though still a little imbalanced, as some words are still more likely to happen than others). A lower temperature means some words are less likely to get picked than others – if you divide a number by a large number it gets really close to zero, and a number close to zero has low probability of being picked.

Alternatively, you can choose the number of words on the wheel which should have high probability – which is done by top-k sampling, or the number of words that should constitute a certain amount of the mass of the wheel – that number is determined by top-p (nucleus) sampling.

