Most success stories of distributed training with TensorFlow have so far been mostly about the aggregation of model updates among the network of workers. The most common approach to training over a cluster is indeed data parallelism, where one worker reads a batch of data, compute gradients, and share them with other workers using AllReduce. Other schemes are possible, and this article discusses distributed-pipeline-input-training@Master. It's a technique where several workers perform data prefetching and computation of the loss function on a different batch of data. At the beginning and at the end of each step, a final worker computes gradients and updates the independent variables, again using AllReduce to aggregate the updates from all the workers. This scheme requires less bandwidth than data parallelism AllReduce but updates the final variables less often.

