 How do they affect text generation? What considerations should we take when evaluating models based on stop sequences?

All generation APIs take a simple input, user-supplied text, and produce a simple output, a sequence of tokens. Users can control this output by interacting with the model while it is generating text, potentially constraining or otherwise modifying the output in interesting ways based on the text that the model has produced so far.

The most common choice, and one that many clients of Hugging Face’s TensorFlow autocomplete API use, is to use stop sequences to control the model’s output. In this article, we investigate how stop sequences work, and other implementation decisions that directly affect control of model generation.

# Text Generation on GPUs

Just like the CPU accomplishes computation via elementary operations like addition or multiplication of scalar numbers, GPUs accomplish parallel computation by performing elementary operations on low level objects called execution streams. A stream contains instructions for hardware to execute, shuffled into a queue. These instructions apply operations to blocks of memory called tiles (either scalar or array), adding, multiplying, etc.

Modern programming for GPUs using libraries like CUDA, cuDNN, or TensorFlow abstracts these details away from the programmer. But it is helpful to keep their existence in mind when you want to accelerate text generation on GPUs.

GPUs are quite good at executing instructions for batched similar operations, but model batching in neural networks is not the only issue. Instructions to copy tiles are queued in a separate stream and execute asynchronously. This means that when threads of the GPU are done copying from one memory location to another (e.g., from host memory, or GPU VRAM to host memory), it may try to start only slicing data without waiting for all copy instructions to finish. This can cause all sorts of race and memory errors.

To speed up text generation, users can prepare data on a separate stream that concurrently feeds the GPU while the model is preparing partial sequences to provide supervision.

# Stop Sequences

Let’s look at a theoretical example of a sequence-to-sequence model. A sequence-to-sequence model takes as input a sequence with length `seq_len` and produces a sequence with, `seq_len_t`. We define the `start_token` and `end_token` IDs respectively. In this framework, input to the model is a sequence of IDs called tokens, and the output shares that overlap. This is similar to how BERT encoder models limit input size to a 512 sub-word token vocabulary, or the decoder as in the case of GPT-2.

Given the following sequence:

```
- word1
- word2
- word3
-
<s>
word4
word5
</s>
```

a sample generation progression would be:

```
- word1	    [0, 1, 2, 3, 4] ->   [4]
- word1 word2   [0, 1, 2, 3] ->   [3, 4]
				
- word1 word2 word3  [0, 1, 2] -> [2, 3, 4]
			
- word1 word2 word3 word4		[1, 2] -> [1, 2, 3, 4]
```

This framework tries to duplicate the interaction that real users of the model would have. Real users prepare a string that is fulfilled by the model into a completion. This approach reduces the number of comparisons to single sequence comparisons. However, this can be very costly since for each additional token a user provides, a whole new sequence is prepared.

Once the prompt is submitted the model needs to build the context. First, a slice of the input vocabulary must be created. The sequence `[0, 1, 2, ...]` contains all tokens starting with the `start_token` and has length `seq_len` is created. Then, another slice of tokens condensed into a sequence of length `seq_len - seq_len_t` must be created. Finally the actual sequence `[4]`, that is a slice of the full sequence `[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]` must be created.

Then as tokens are added to the sequence, the shape of the employed slice must change, since the sequence of tokens for the `attention_mask` changes too.
