 Here’s an explanation that I picked up yesterday from Dick Yue; note that teacher and student are not amused:

Students make mistakes; that goes without saying. Teachers too. Usually I misspell words and confuse names, but here’s something more subtle.

Last week I was explaining the similarities between the Orthogonal Matching Pursuit (OMP) algorithm from compressed sensing and matching pursuit from sparse approximation, when I suddenly discovered that there’s a complication. The basic idea is that if you have a signal dictionary and want to find the atom that best matches a signal, you run through a greedy search and select the atom that gives you the biggest component of your signal in that dictionary. You then subtract the atom multiplied by the projection coefficient and repeat, in the hope of finding the next biggest component. Throw away overlaps and iterate, and in the limit (if the signal is sparse) you essentially find the atoms that gave rise to the signal.

And that was when I explained OMP, until last week…

Earlier in the same lecture, I had discussed the basics of the orthogonal projection, when a student asked “what is the greedy search then?”. And therein lies the rub:

in OMP, the atom is simply the column of a matrix (think of an oversampled DFT, or union of bases). The greedy component is selecting the column that maximizes the inner product, and then subtracting this component from any remaining part of the signal. This process is then repeated on the remaining signal. It’s rather simple actually.

In matching pursuit, on the other hand, the atoms are themselves functions (e.g. complex exponentials, wavelets, and the like). The greedy search here is two-fold and considered the biggest obstacle of the process. First, for each possible translation of the atom, we get a different inner product. Second, for each possible scaling of this translation we get a different inner product. (Think of translating a complex exponential or wavelet along a signal and multiplying by some number.)

The inner product is largest when both the translational and scaling aspects match with the signal. Once we find these, we strip the atom from the signal, and continue the process on the residual. This gives us a very powerful method of reconstructing signals that are sparse in some basis or dictionary that we may not immediately have in hand.

We can then say that OMP is a special case of matching pursuit in which both translational and scaling amounts are given a cartesian grid, which we run through exactly.
