The dropout layer is another building block of neural networks. Here’s an example of how the dropout layer can be used within a network:

- Input 1x784 (28x28) – Convolution 32 3x3 — Batch norm—ReLU—Dropout
- Max pool 2×2
- Convolution 32 3x3 — Batch norm—ReLU—Dropout — Max pool 2×2
- Convolution 64 3x3 — Batch norm—ReLU
- Dropout
- Flatten
- Dense 64 — Batch norm—ReLU—Dropout
- Dense 10

Notice the presence of dropout blocks after the convolutional blocks on the first two lines and after the densely-connected layers. What’s the goal? It’s to reduce overfitting, thus helping your network generalize better.

Here’s a nice diagram showing schematically how the dropout layer works:

This layer randomly sets some of the inputs to zero — i.e. it ignores them. The probability of an input being kept is a hyperparameter. 0 means dropout (= ignore input) and 1 means no dropout. For example, p=0.75 means that each input has a 75% chance of being kept, hence a 25% chance of being dropped (= ignoring input).

The idea behind dropout layers is to force the network to learn a combination of features rather than rely on one or a few. You can select better weight matrices by making the connection between the neurons sparse and unpredictable. If a network manages to learn a desired output with randomly dropped input values, it will more likely work with real data too. The brain conceptually does the same thing with a “dropout” of neurons.

So dropout layers help achieve better generalization of your model by selecting a few random features and ignoring the rest. The goal of this layer is to make the network overfit less. It can be used when training a Keras sequential API model.

