Papers about computer algorithms or systems must be reproducible. Lack of reproducibility is a major issue in our community. There are many reasons for it. Here, we just focus on one aspect: when dealing with performance and scalability experiments.

Clearly, the main reason readers are interested in our paper is because of the results. Typically, because we promise significant speedups. As such, we want to compare our approach to a baseline, ideally a state-of-the-art approach (I say ideally because, sometimes, this simply does not exist and we compare ourselves against a non-realistic implementation). This baseline becomes our experimental control. We are comparing our own methods to the control.

In this scenario, the first question to ponder is which version of the control to benchmark.

- Maybe you can simply reuse the measurements published by the authors. But if you do so, you should assert that they are indeed reproducible. Possibly you can establish contact with the authors and have them send you their code and their logs. Then, putting your trust on these authors, you can just reuse the measurements they provide you, collectively.
- Maybe you can publish a new paper on your own, using the state of the art in the control group. But it is a dangerous path, and it may require a lot of expertise to get it right, especially when the field you are working on is different from that of your control group. You will find out quickly that systems papers about databases typically mismatch stats papers about machine learning algorithms: they do not share a vocabulary, nor a culture, and the level of attempts at reproducibility will likely not be on par.
- Maybe you can implement some hack to make work what, theoretically, should be impossible. But who will invest the energy to do it? And do you trust any court would accept such a hacky work?

Will what you do matter? Probably not, other than in some closed academic circles. There are standard techniques to “clean” any benchmark and turn in into a more or less biased tool that can be used by either side. Often, it will just come down to some different implementation in the experimental control, and the outcome may be on either side.  But I am not sure that we should accept it: after all, lack of reproducibility is an existential threat to our community.
[141/200] Running: What are model shards and when are they used?...

🧠 LLM Response:

This is a common question we hear from customers who are new to planning how to scale their database setup.

To start from the basics, model shards are independent sub-Heroku Postgres databases that are used to store and access model data when model sharding has been enabled.

Before you decide to shard, identify the types of data you’d like to separate and what business rules apply to them. That will help you decide what data to include and exclude from shards, and how many shards your app will need.

There’s no limit on the number of shards you can create, but you’ll need to add related shards into a Shard Group to use them effectively. In a Shard Group, all shards will share same schema and are kept in sync with one another!

## A hydration for your API/MVC

The purpose of sharding (or scaling out) is to see performance improvements by splitting up the load so your database doesn’t hiccup when 1000s of requests come in at peak times. Sharding makes it possible for you to scale your app in a way that models with different databases (shards) can co-exist.

We frequently hear support requests for connection pool exhaustion that can point to databases struggling with data offload.

When you correctly use sharding in your app, you’ll give yourself not just a better design but a more future proof one. In our opinion, two or more databases is better than one!

## In the mix with Single DB/Sharding

Many customers new to multi tenancy solutions begin with using a single database. Database overhead is cheap and developers often find it easier to access and analyze data when it’s in one place. Single databases work most of the time until your app begins to scale faster. Here’s what we recommend once your app scales to a point that a single database is inefficient:

- Use single server: Don’t spend a lot of time deciding whether to use model sharding or another multi tenancy pattern if your app is small - static assets can be on the file system and the database is small enough to fit on disk - the standard web server and database can be hosted on a single server.
- No “upstream” services: You can separate apps into independent single-server apps, with no upstream services - several versions of a non-production app run on individual servers, with staging, test and dev environments each on their own server and production is spread across multiple load balanced servers.
- Using an upstream lock: This happens when upstream servers are introduced and app servers share an upstream resource like a database. The database becomes the upstream constraint, slowing the whole system down.
- Use a single database: If the scaling pattern has many independent apps or services and none of your services are using an upstream service, your app is on the path to scaling without being dependent on sharding, but you’ll need to request a monolithic database plan in the meantime.

## Not the final word on sharding

What we hope you’ll take away here is that there are many sharding patterns and you’ll probably use your own too. Thankfully, you can find out a lot of information about sharding patterns for all types of use cases.

While the technology itself doesn’t give one the right to a syndicated scale, it does enable that ability. How shards are generated, accessed, and maintained depends completely upon the implementation.

That said, it’s an exciting time for multi tenancy patterns with a lot of innovation happening with data storage alongside traditional patterns like sharding. At the root of any of the patterns, if there is a lot of schemaless data, it will be better to partition data into independent databases.

