🧠 LLM Response:


2020-12-17 by Jan Ivar Henriksen

For predictive models to be impactful it is not enough to have high quality model predictions. It is also important that predictions are available when you need them. This makes it relevant to track and measure how quickly your can expect a prediction from your model. One way of doing this is to look at what is called a server cold start. So... what is a cold start and why is how should I approach this if I have my model deployed through the superwise platform?

When you bring ML into production, you want to make sure your models are ready to provide predictions when you need them. Often those predictions are needed in near-real-time. As a examples, suppose you have a classification model and your API can make predictions so fast that you can give a prediction within milliseconds – but what happens in the overall system if it takes minutes from you make your API call until your API returns the prediction? Then those predictions are not useful. So in order track and monitor if the predictions are returned in a timely manner, it may be beneficial to track some metrics around how fast these predictions are.

## What is a server cold start and a server optimization

One simple metric you can track is to measure the latency between the invocation of the function and when the response is returned. I.e. how long does it take before the first prediction is returned. A server cold start is when you measure the first call (or subsequent calls after a period of inactivity). This metric will often be higher than for the subsequent calls. The reason for this is that the prediction function is often pre-warmed (cached) is some manner by the server. The cold start is likely to also vary between different vendors servers. The latest type of servers provided by AWS, called AWS Lambda layers solves this by keeping the servers warm even when idle to avoid this. So measuring severity and see the cold start latency helps you to make some optimization around how fast it takes before the first prediction is returned. At least it flags when there is something that maybe deserves to be looked at.

## How does superwise calculate this

We offer built-in tracking of the health and performance of your API, but we plan to add more features in this space. One example of this that will be coming soon is various visualizations where you can see the p99 performance of your API, and in some ways monitoring the tails in the distribution in terms of modeling, but also compute time for these predictions. So definitely something to be added soon. Please let us know if superwise should work on any particular metrics for you.

## How should I approach this cold start during development and production

Cause especially if you do not have the resources yourself to deal with this, this might be tricky. One thing is to be smart about which physical location of your prediction servers you have your functions. If you notice that the cold starts are really slow, the problem may be that the geographical distance between your machine and API provider may be too high. Then it is good to have explicit policies.

In addition, if possible, you may choose a cloud provider that has prediction servers in your location or in a place that can provide you with adequate speed. The way this works in AWS is that you have servers in different regions, which you can choose. But for performance reasons, you may want to keep this within the area where you expect the API to be called the most.

Another practical thing to do is to warm them up by calling the API occasionally, so you hint to the AWS Lambda functions that the function is important, without actually fetching predictions. But this really comes down to how important it is to you that it returns fast as it conceals that you have a problem during the cold start that you do not solve. You will also be able to set alert thresholds based on latency to check if the prediction ever arrives within any period of time at all.
