## Solution:

Performance issues in Oracle VLDBs often have their root in one or more of the six VLDB performance killers:
⦁ Full Table Scan
⦁ Full Index Scans
⦁ Memory Mismanagement
⦁ Shared Pool Miss
⦁ I/O Mismanagement
⦁ Padding Inefficiencies
Of these, problems with padding inefficiencies are one of the easier performance issues to resolve (although they may not always be noticed).

DBAs sometimes say it should not matter how a table is decomposed. They also say it should not matter how the table’s indexes are decomposed, and they say that row movement in tables can resolve all table-size issues. As you grow a VLDB (and when table scans and index scans are the performance problems), then I suggest you NOT follow the recommendations noted above (from the DBAs who say it shouldn’t matter how the table is decomposed).
For partitioned tables and indexes, it is better to not have 1000 of these (say 1 row per partition). Instead, one should not look only to partition columns, but also to ERD/composite/hybrid partitioning. As per Oracle, no more than 2000 of these is best (I feel 500 or fewer is really best).

Further, in all dimensions (not only indexes) one should avoid having extremely wide column sizes (which both cause wasted PCTFREE space and cause excessive, improper chaining/migration). Instead, fixed-size small columns can be optimal. Also avoid UUID and other long codes. Use shorter codes with UUID translations and long lookup codes with minimum large sizes.
PCTUSED can very often be re-set to zero for VLDB tables. It can sometimes best not be set if reclaiming space is more important than CHAINING/MIGRATION.
PCTFREE must often be completely or almost completely eliminated. But this may cause excessive CHAINING. It can be alleviated by more targeted partition sizes, and small minimized column sizes. Set PCTUSED too low and there can be excessive CHAINING.

Attempts to lower CHAINING may lead to too much space consumption. So PCTUSED may need raising (but not over ten percent ever) to hold space for CHAINED data, which can be caused by UPDATES, INSERTS/BLH INDEPENDENT DELETES, and unwanted unused space in the CHAINED data.
PCTFREE can best be set to a smaller number than PCTUSED. This could be a number like PCTFREE-5 and PCTUSED=5. But even that may enable as much space utilization as is preferred.
By default, the varcharsize increments every 2 3 bytes in size (1, 4, 7, 10,13, etc.). The user can re-set this increment size.
To avoid the CHAINING variance otherwise often found in a VLDB, use minimum W/M vsize, and set UPDATE=In-Place EXACTLY. And avoid row movement.
By that I mean long rows that need be decomposed into structures that have short data lengths. Avoid long UUIDs, and long CHAR(N) codes.
Avoid increasing the VARCHAR length more than necessary to retain minimal most required sizes in VARCHAR storage (which may require adjusting the varcharsize increment). Don’t use varchar columns that have minimum row sizes of over 100, and try to NOT have the average column max size be over 100.
There is never an end to trying to avoid this last problem. It can substantially increase or decrease IO/CPU/Memory utilization and collectively get the VLDB sizing right.
