Model size is determined by a combination of the neural network architecture, model weights precision, and quantization scheme. Larger neural networks, e.g. VGG-16, tend to have lower weights precision, 16 bits and 8 bits, and low quantization, 4 bits and 2 bits. Smaller neural networks, e.g. MobileNet, tend to have higher quantization and weights precision.

Fully connected layers and convolutional neural networks show better accuracy when weights precision and quantization are large, and thus the model size increases. The model characteristics such as the number of layers, types and sizes of filters, number of channels, and number of convolutional or fully connected layers, will also determine the model’s size and influence accuracy and inference latency. Mainly, the larger the model is the larger compute and power demands to process an image and the slower the inference can be, at a given processor speed. There are model size compression techniques that aim to reduce the above issues.

Higher rates of quantization, impact of using shallow neural architectures, and lower bit width of parameters in common neural architecture designs can save a large amount of total resources. It is then key to find the optimal compromise to match deployment hardware resources, and it would be ideal to have the adapted neural architecture for efficient implementation on the given hardware i.e. reduce the neural network depth by a specific amount taking into account the appropriate number of filters. Since model size is one of the variables used for neural architecture search space selection, an algorithmic method would be beneficial to reduce the deep neural architecture’s complexity and adapt it to match the hardware inference capabilities and constraints while guarding accuracy.
