 In statistics, sampling is the process of selecting observations from a given population. Once the population is sampled, information is drawn from the limited sample to make conclusions about the whole population. The two methods of sampling are probability, where selected samples are chosen based on the principles of probability, and non-probability, where selected samples are chosen based on convenience. For the probability method, there are additional sampling approaches that could apply.

## Temperature vs Randomness in Sampling

### Simple Random Sampling

This method is best used on populations that are homogenous. The population can be represented with its individual values (depending on the size) and simple random sampling involves picking individuals in a way that does not follow any pattern (selected values are independent of other values in the sample). Usually this is done by using a computer-generated program to select random values.

### Sampling With Replacement

We can think of this as sampling with a potential for the same observations to be repeatedly selected. However, the same observation cannot be selected over and over in a finite population because as the sample size is increased, it approaches the population size. The probability of selecting a certain value or individual from the population the second time is P_{1}P_{1}, the probability of selecting it the third time is P_{1}P_{1}P_{1}, and so on. Although the population may be finite in this sample, sampling with replacement is most utilized when the population is very large.

### Random Sample

Random sample means the sample includes every possible outcome an equal number of times. For example, to select different bodies of water, a researcher might pick from a list of water sources separated into lakes, rivers, and oceans. If the researcher actually has one for each category, then the results would be classified as a random sample. The samples are independent of each other in this type of sampling.

### Systematic Sample

This example of temperature vs randomness in sampling refers to the process where only a random starting point is chosen while subsequent members of the sample are selected using a fixed periodic interval. A list could be used to select one individual (based on the interval needed), and then the sample is created by selecting every every individual listed after the starting point.

### Stratified Sample

This differs from systematic in that every member of the sample is selected randomly from a different stratum. Strata (plural for stratum) are comprised of sub-populations, containing individuals that are similar. For example, if a statistics professor needed to pick students to serve as group leaders for various assignments, that professor could stratify the class by the students’ final exam scores.

Then, the professor would randomly sample by picking one student from the sub-population of students with scores under 59, one student from the sub-population with scores between 60 and 69 and so on, one student from the subpopulation with scores between 90 and 99, and one student from the subpopulation with scores between 100 and 100. By doing this, the professor would have a stratified sample.
