# What is Transfer Learning?

Transfer learning is a technique in machine learning that allows us to leverage pretrained models to improve model performance on a new target task with either: 1. more easily available/unlabelled data 2. less easily available/labelled data

I’ll be elaborating on these points 1&2 later in the post. The models could be CNN’s (for computer vision tasks), RNN’s (for NLP tasks), etc.

The main focus of this section is understanding the intuition behind transfer learning. Let’s start with some visualizations from ImageNet to help us understand the motivation behind transfer learning.

> There are about 30,000 images per class in the ImageNet visual recognition challenge. There are 1,000 categories (like Yoga vs Skateboard vs Pugs), and over 14 million labelled images in total. — Jose Angelo Esteves Franca (link here)

# Intuition

## Goals of transfer learning

1. Leveraging pretrained models to help the model learn new classes.
    - e,g. The model is trained on a subset of 1000 object categories from the sunset dataset containing about 1000 classes in total. The model is then used on a new set of ~100 object categories. The pretrained model weights can potentially help us in learning the new set of object categories, even without having to start to train a model from scratch on the new set.
    - This kind of intuition is useful because most of the basic level higher classes share similar properties. Even while learning a new language, the English language serves as a prior to learn other new languages easily.
2. Leveraging models to learn new tasks (without having to retrain the model from scratch).
    - e.g. take pretrained encoder and decoder’s decoding representations from the framework instead of both the encoding and decoding layers and make any changes if necessary. Retrain the new output representation and use this model for the new target task (example in later sections). The training dataset for this new target task could be unlabelled data.

## Layers/Environment specific characteristics

Layers in deep convolutional networks hold their specific place for certain things. Early layers mainly capture primitive features, the middle layers capture high-level features and the final layers consist of classifier, i.e., they take the output of conv layers and map the given input to the likely classes in target.

For example, early layers tend to be faster and will converge more quickly. High-level features differ greatly within datasets and depend heavily on the immediate inputs (eg. spatial patterns at the pixel level). Later layers tend to converge much more slowly. Higher-level features are more dependent on the dataset/ task.

If we recall this dataset from Andrej Karpathy’s very famous functional program tail recursion CS231n example image below, which highlights the difference in if-features from when-features!

The if-features would work for discrimination across big classes (dog vs tree) while the when features are very specific to the task and require tight learning of dataset specific information (do we have a wreck or rescue dog situation to go by?).

## Take a look at the Feature maps in VGG-16

This helps us in understanding that a middle-out approach to converging in learning is better than a top-down approach. It is helpful if one understands this in practice. Even though we apply transfer learning in a top-down fashion (starting from top layers then working outwards), the underlying learning happens in a middle out approach. The final model will first try to learn some features, then some more low-level features because the early layers actually learn down to the small details even if you tune them, and once those are firmed up, then the model works it’s way outwards to the final top down prediction.

