Breakdown of Common Components of a Convolutional Neural Network (CNN):

- Input

    - The input can be a single image, a convo2d input layer, or the image dimensions. These images might be a black and white picture, which would be one image, or if the image in colored, there would be three inputs for each pixel (the three input layers for each of the image channels) And so the convo input formats might be given as: `shape([width, height, depth])` e.g. for RGB `shape([200, 200, 3])`
- Convolution Layer

    - The convolution layer is the core component of any CNN architecture. Convolution itself is not just a component thrown into CNNs randomly, there was a lot of thought put behind it, largely based on the Natural Processing of biology, adapted in its architecture and the idea of local receptive fields. Ideally, the filters are used to find the key features (corner, edges or texture) that help the CNN detect shapes. This process of “convoluting” or filtering the image might look like the following (take note that this step is taken only along the two-dimensional words, aka the x and y-axis, as it does not include the channel dimension. And so for each pixel, there are 3 different values, one from each filter pass and these pixels will be layered atop of each other.)
- Non-Linearity

    - The non-linearity layer is based on a simple idea that most worldly complex things can’t exactly fit the linearity of a straight line. Instead, if there are curves, tight turns, large bends, etc., it will skew the image, resulting in more bad results no matter how many layers are added on. There are a few type of non-linearities that can be used, such as the sigmoid function, tanh(x) function, and others, although the ReLU is currently the most commonly used function in the non-linearity layer for various reasons.
- Pooling

    - You could say pooling is its own type of filter. This filter comes in various names and forms as in max pooling, average pooling, and even sum pooling. Pooling was introduced to achieve some translational invariance, although at the cost of size (the pooled output size is not only a little less blurry but also more condensed than the image that goes into pooling). By condensing, we can help preserve information flow and helps every feature learned, rather than just the most important features. For max pooling, we tend to take the max value of each subregion, but average and sum pooling do what they suggest—take the average and sum values of each subregion.
- Fully Connected Layer

    - The fully connected layer, like the non-linearity layer, is present in most neural networks. In this layer, every input is connected to every possible output, with all the nodes of 2 consecutive computations are fully connected. Fully connected is like a classification layer, where it tends to bundle up all the information gathered from a bunch of small regions and understand how those individual parts fit as a whole at a much more global level than the convolution or pooling parts. This layer also tends to reduce the size from its original convoluted form and is important in associating a label with our inputs.

