## Fine Tuning An Existing Model

The easiest way to focus an existing model on a specific use case is fine-tuning. As an example, let’s imagine a company named Dr. Steve Appointment App wants to fine tune a model to help them automate the scheduling of patient appointments and includes the following requirements:

- App must be able to understand the local climate of a patient’s city to help avoid scheduling too many patients on potentially snowy or rainy days.
- App must be able to ask the patient questions, listen to their responses, and take appropriate actions based on what the customer said.
- App must be able to list all appointments for a specific date and time slot.
- App must be able to recommend a specific date/time for an appointment, and if it’s not available, suggest the next closest open slot.
- App must be able to send the appointment as a calendar invitation and send the patient a reminder the day before the appointment.

In order to make this small application work, we would first need to find an existing NLP model more focused on the q-and-a style we’re looking for, a model like BlenderBot that functions more like an old school chatbot would fit best here. Once we’ve selected our model, we would just need some data to define our own specific corpora for training and fine-tuning.

The process typically works like this:

For the data, we need to feed the model sometime from an existing corpus. Depending on your use case, corpuses are available online for free, such as reddit, twitter, news articles, and more. After that, any custom data we decide to add, such as patient appointment related examples, will go into fine-tuning.

### Fine-Tuning Metrics​

All fine-tuning data and parameters are decided by the model’s owner. It is important to code up different types of metrics as machine learning models goal is to get lower and lower loss. For language models, human generated data is considered the highest quality. Chat-based examples can come from customer service interactions, as well as product reviews. It is worth mentioning that the quality of the “labels” that are generated for a machine learning model are directly related to the quality of your results. This means that the data itself is important, but the labels your application mark as the correct answers are more important.

